Third-party material

This release is the authors' own implementation of the work described in
"Ontology-Guided Opportunistic CT Agent for Early Osteoporosis Prognosis Using
Vertebral Attenuation, Trabecular Texture, and Fracture-Risk Semantics". It bundles no
third-party code, no third-party model weights and no third-party data. The resources
below are named because the implementation interoperates with them or because the
preprocessing described in the manuscript depends on them. Copyright in each remains
with its authors.

Datasets

1. CTSpine1K, a large-scale computed-tomography spinal vertebrae segmentation collection.
   Repository: https://github.com/MIRACLE-Center/CTSpine1K
   Paper:      Deng et al., Machine Learning for Biomedical Imaging, MICCAI Open Data
               2024-2025, 824-832 (2025).
   Licence:    openly distributed; consult the repository's own terms before
               redistribution. Not redistributed here.
   Role:       anatomical pre-training for the localisation stage.

2. TotalSegmentator, segmentations of 104 anatomical structures in computed tomography.
   Repository: https://github.com/wasserth/TotalSegmentator
   Dataset:    https://zenodo.org/records/22688904
   Paper:      Wasserthal et al., Radiology: Artificial Intelligence 5, e230024 (2023).
   Licence:    the dataset is published under a Creative Commons Attribution licence;
               consult the record's own terms before redistribution. Not redistributed
               here.
   Role:       reference organ definition for abdominal localisation in preprocessing.

3. VerSe, the large-scale vertebrae segmentation challenge collection.
   Repository: https://github.com/anjany/verse
   Dataset:    https://osf.io/nqjyw/
   Record:     https://zenodo.org/records/3759104
   Licence:    distributed on request through the challenge page; consult the challenge
               rules and the record terms before redistribution. Not redistributed here.
   Role:       protocol-stress component for the localisation stage.

Terminologies

4. The concept layer is informed by four published sources. Their full releases are
   governed by their own licences and are not bundled.

   SNOMED CT (SNOMED International). Licence-gated in most jurisdictions; the
   implementation does not redistribute any SNOMED CT content. Browser:
   https://browser.ihtsdotools.org/

   RadLex (Radiological Society of North America). Term browser:
   https://www.radlex.org/RID/

   LOINC (Regenstrief Institute). The release is published at loinc.org; no content from
   it is redistributed or copied here.

   DICOM Structured Reporting, PS3.16 coded terminology (National Electrical
   Manufacturers Association). Public specification:
   https://dicom.nema.org/medical/dicom/current/output/chtml/part16/chapter_A.html

   The shipped index carries release-local keys of the form OSTEOCT-nnnn, each recording
   the source system and the display term. Live identifier resolution against the
   licensed releases is not performed.

Standards referenced by the axiom families

5. ISCD Official Positions and the WHO T-score band definitions supply the diagnostic
   band axioms; FRAX supplies the risk-tier semantics; the Genant semiquantitative
   grading supplies the severity axioms. These are clinical standards, not datasets; no
   material from them is redistributed, and the thresholds are stated as method
   constants in configs/ontology/osteoct.yaml.

Software

The implementation depends on PyTorch, NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn and PyYAML, all under
their own licences, and on pytest, ruff, black, isort and mypy as development tooling.
Optional support for DICOM Part-10 encoding uses pydicom when it is installed. No source
from those projects is copied into this repository; they are consumed as installed
packages.

Clinical cohort

The multicentre cohort (sites S1-S3 across two regions) is not third-party material and
is not distributed. It is held under the data-usage terms of the contributing
institutions, its records cannot be released at record level, and the manuscript's
data-availability statement says the same. The cohort-level quantities reported in the
evaluation output are produced by the schema-compatible cohort fixture shipped with this
release, never from the study cohort, and no manuscript table value is claimed from it.
